{"recipe": "ellipse-subgroup-fpr", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "70178f2", "kind": "false-positive rate on known-human ESL student writing, by subgroup", "corpus": "ELLIPSE train split (Crossley et al. 2023), CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, 3904 essays >=60 words", "tier": "lite", "argv": ["-m", "eval.subgroup_audit", "--corpus", "ellipse", "--tier", "lite", "--n", "0", "--sweep"], "rows": [{"threshold": 0.3, "overall_fpr": 0.9744, "overall_ci": [0.9689, 0.9789], "saturated": true, "separated_axes": []}, {"threshold": 0.5, "overall_fpr": 0.3868, "overall_ci": [0.3716, 0.4022], "saturated": false, "separated_axes": []}, {"threshold": 0.7, "overall_fpr": 0.032, "overall_ci": [0.0269, 0.038], "saturated": false, "separated_axes": []}, {"threshold": 0.8, "overall_fpr": 0.0079, "overall_ci": [0.0056, 0.0112], "saturated": true, "separated_axes": []}, {"threshold": 0.9, "overall_fpr": 0.0013, "overall_ci": [0.0005, 0.003], "saturated": true, "separated_axes": []}, {"threshold": 0.95, "overall_fpr": 0.0005, "overall_ci": [0.0001, 0.0019], "saturated": true, "separated_axes": []}, {"threshold": 0.99, "overall_fpr": 0.0003, "overall_ci": [0.0, 0.0014], "saturated": true, "separated_axes": []}], "note": "FIRST RUN of the audit position (docs/strategy-the-audit-position.md). Two findings. (1) untell's OWN lite tier flags 97.4% of known-human ESL student essays at its shipped 0.30 threshold; the documented lite figure was 30% on conversational prose. (2) NO subgroup ratio separated at 95% confidence at any threshold, so this corpus shows a catastrophic overall false-positive rate and NO demonstrated per-group disparity. At 0.30 the detector is saturated and disparity is unmeasurable rather than absent, which is why the sweep exists."}
{"recipe": "ellipse-fpr-by-proficiency", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "c2a4c80", "kind": "false-positive rate by rated English proficiency, known-human ESL essays", "corpus": "ELLIPSE train split, 3904 essays >=60 words, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "tier": "lite", "argv": ["-m", "eval.subgroup_audit", "--corpus", "ellipse", "--tier", "lite", "--n", "0", "--by", "Overall", "--threshold", "0.5"], "rows": [{"threshold": 0.5, "overall_fpr": 0.3868, "by_proficiency": {"2": 0.3373, "2.5": 0.3396, "3": 0.3697, "3.5": 0.4273, "4": 0.4539, "4.5": 0.5306}, "disparity": {"worst": "4.5", "worst_fpr": 0.5306, "best": "2", "best_fpr": 0.3373, "ratio": 1.57, "separated": true}}, {"threshold": 0.7, "overall_fpr": 0.032, "by_proficiency": {"2": 0.0621, "2.5": 0.0377, "3": 0.0318, "3.5": 0.0191, "4": 0.0253, "4.5": 0.0306}, "disparity": {"worst": "2", "worst_fpr": 0.0621, "best": "3.5", "best_fpr": 0.0191, "ratio": 3.25, "separated": true}}], "note": "The axis that separates. At threshold 0.50 the lite tier's FPR rises MONOTONICALLY across all six reportable proficiency levels, 33.7% -> 53.1%, worst at the HIGHEST proficiency, 1.57x, Wilson intervals separate. That inverts the literature's perplexity story, in which low-proficiency writers are flagged most: a tells-based detector keys on polish, so the most fluent learners look most machine-like to it. At threshold 0.70 the ordering is NOT monotonic and the worst group flips to the LOWEST proficiency (6.2% vs 1.9%, 3.25x, separated). So which group a detector fails depends on where its threshold is set, and a single-threshold audit would have reported one direction and missed the reversal."}
{"recipe": "ellipse-lite-signal-decomposition", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "c115a26", "kind": "which half of perplexity_burstiness drives the proficiency disparity", "corpus": "ELLIPSE train split, 3904 essays >=60 words", "tier": "lite", "rows": [{"proficiency": "2", "n": 338, "common_word_ratio": 0.6056, "burstiness_cv": 0.5187, "mean_words": 354}, {"proficiency": "2.5", "n": 689, "common_word_ratio": 0.605, "burstiness_cv": 0.5188, "mean_words": 394}, {"proficiency": "3", "n": 1385, "common_word_ratio": 0.6006, "burstiness_cv": 0.4978, "mean_words": 421}, {"proficiency": "3.5", "n": 784, "common_word_ratio": 0.5846, "burstiness_cv": 0.465, "mean_words": 457}, {"proficiency": "4", "n": 553, "common_word_ratio": 0.5794, "burstiness_cv": 0.4514, "mean_words": 488}, {"proficiency": "4.5", "n": 98, "common_word_ratio": 0.5633, "burstiness_cv": 0.4156, "mean_words": 533}], "note": "Both components fall monotonically with proficiency and they pull OPPOSITE ways. Common-word ratio falling means richer vocabulary, which reads as MORE human and protects stronger writers -- the perplexity account in the literature, behaving as predicted. Burstiness CV falling means more uniform sentence lengths, which reads as MORE machine-like. Burstiness wins: FPR rises with proficiency. So the burstiness term penalises writing maturity. Corrects an earlier claim in the strategy doc that the lite tier was tells-keyed rather than perplexity-keyed; it is perplexity_burstiness and is exactly what it says."}
{"recipe": "ellipse-proficiency-replication", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "29b76b1", "kind": "held-out replication of the proficiency false-positive gap", "tier": "lite", "corpus": "ELLIPSE train (3904) vs held-out test split (2571), CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "rows": [{"split": "train", "band_low_n": 1056, "band_low_fpr": 0.332, "band_high_n": 1463, "band_high_fpr": 0.442, "ratio": 1.33, "separated": true}, {"split": "held-out", "band_low_n": 673, "band_low_fpr": 0.348, "band_high_n": 945, "band_high_fpr": 0.43, "ratio": 1.24, "separated": true}], "note": "Banded low (<=2.5) vs high (>=3.5) proficiency at threshold 0.50. Overall FPR was 38.7% on BOTH splits and per-level rates tracked within a point wherever n>300. The gap separates at 95% on both. NOTE what did NOT replicate: strict monotonicity across all six levels, and separation of the top level 4.5 alone (n=55 held-out, ratio 1.42x, intervals overlap). The banded contrast is the claim the data supports; the six-level monotonic version was an artifact of the larger split's sample sizes."}
{"recipe": "ellipse-component-ablation", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "29b76b1", "kind": "which half of perplexity_burstiness is biased, and in which direction", "tier": "lite", "corpus": "ELLIPSE train (n=2519 banded) and held-out (n=1618 banded)", "rows": [{"split": "train", "component": "vocabulary", "low_fpr": 0.634, "high_fpr": 0.403, "worst": "low", "ratio": 1.57, "separated": true}, {"split": "train", "component": "burstiness", "low_fpr": 0.402, "high_fpr": 0.571, "worst": "high", "ratio": 1.42, "separated": true}, {"split": "held-out", "component": "vocabulary", "low_fpr": 0.637, "high_fpr": 0.402, "worst": "low", "ratio": 1.59, "separated": true}, {"split": "held-out", "component": "burstiness", "low_fpr": 0.415, "high_fpr": 0.561, "worst": "high", "ratio": 1.35, "separated": true}], "note": "THE RESULT. Each component thresholded at its own median so both flag ~half the corpus (equal power). The two halves of ONE detector are biased in OPPOSITE directions against OPPOSITE groups, both separated at 95%, on both splits. Vocabulary penalises LOW-proficiency writers, which is exactly Liang et al.'s perplexity account. Burstiness penalises HIGH-proficiency writers, which the literature has not isolated. They partly cancel in the combined score, so ANY aggregate fairness number for this detector understates both -- and a benchmark that treats the detector as a black box cannot see it. Exposed via `untell-subgroup-audit --ablate`."}
{"recipe": "ellipse-raw-signal-effect-sizes", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "a63ebb9", "kind": "do the RAW features carry proficiency, before any detector calibrates them", "corpus": "ELLIPSE train (low 1056 / high 1463) and held-out (low 673 / high 945)", "tier": "n/a", "rows": [{"split": "train", "signal": "sentence_length_cv", "low": 0.5214, "high": 0.4555, "cohen_d": -0.394}, {"split": "train", "signal": "common_word_ratio", "low": 0.6045, "high": 0.5803, "cohen_d": -0.476}, {"split": "held-out", "signal": "sentence_length_cv", "low": 0.5179, "high": 0.4567, "cohen_d": -0.363}, {"split": "held-out", "signal": "common_word_ratio", "low": 0.6069, "high": 0.5809, "cohen_d": -0.537}], "note": "The bias is in the FEATURES, not in untell's calibration. More proficient learners are measurably less bursty (d ~ -0.38, replicated) and use measurably fewer common words (d ~ -0.51, replicated). So any detector treating LOW sentence-length variance as machine-like inherits a penalty on writing maturity, and any detector treating predictable vocabulary as machine-like inherits the opposite penalty -- regardless of implementation. SCOPE: burstiness is the feature GPTZero popularised and still explains publicly, but GPTZero itself migrated to a deep-learning architecture in autumn 2023, so this is NOT a claim about current GPTZero. It applies to detectors that still use the heuristic, which includes untell's own lite tier and much of the open-source tier."}
{"recipe": "asap-subgroup-fpr", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "ed68ef3", "kind": "independent-corpus replication: FPR by subgroup on ASAP 2.0", "tier": "lite", "threshold": 0.5, "corpus": "ASAP 2.0 train split (Crossley et al.), 17,307 source-based essays, CC BY 4.0. Independent of ELLIPSE in task type, sampling and population; carries ell_status, which ELLIPSE cannot because every ELLIPSE writer is a learner.", "overall_fpr": 0.316, "rows": [{"axis": "ell_status", "worst": "No (non-ELL)", "worst_fpr": 0.322, "best": "Yes (ELL)", "best_fpr": 0.267, "n_worst": 14798, "n_best": 2269, "ratio": 1.21, "separated": true}, {"axis": "student_disability_status", "worst": "Identified as having disability", "worst_fpr": 0.383, "best": "Not identified", "best_fpr": 0.348, "n_worst": 1921, "n_best": 11367, "ratio": 1.1, "separated": true}, {"axis": "economically_disadvantaged", "worst": "Not economically disadvantaged", "worst_fpr": 0.381, "best": "Economically disadvantaged", "best_fpr": 0.337, "n_worst": 5003, "n_best": 8285, "ratio": 1.13, "separated": true}, {"axis": "race_ethnicity", "worst": "American Indian/Alaskan Native", "worst_fpr": 0.363, "best": "Asian/Pacific Islander", "best_fpr": 0.234, "n_worst": 113, "n_best": 991, "ratio": 1.55, "separated": true, "robust_pair": "White 33.1% (n=6989) vs Asian/Pacific Islander 23.4% (n=991), 1.41x, separated"}], "note": "ALL FOUR axes separate at 95%. The ell_status result INVERTS the dominant account: non-ELL writers are flagged MORE than English language learners (32.2% vs 26.7%), on an independent 17,307-essay corpus, where Liang et al. found commercial detectors flagging non-native TOEFL essays at 61%. Consistent with the ELLIPSE proficiency finding and with the burstiness mechanism: more fluent, more evenly-structured prose reads as machine-like to a burstiness-weighted detector. Three of four axes point the same way -- the more advantaged or more fluent writer is flagged more (non-ELL > ELL, not-disadvantaged > disadvantaged, higher proficiency > lower). Disability is the exception and points the other way. SCOPE: this is a burstiness-heavy heuristic detector, NOT a commercial neural one, so it does not contradict Liang et al. about the tools they tested -- it shows this CLASS of detector carries the opposite bias. Two tool defects were found and fixed producing this: 'NA' was being treated as a subgroup (4,019 rows, made the 'best' arm, 2.01x phantom ratio), and a --csv label filter silently dropped ell_status and rendered an empty axis."}
{"recipe": "burstiness-formulation-robustness", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "2cbe9bc", "kind": "is the burstiness disparity an artifact of one formula", "corpus": "ELLIPSE train (proficiency bands) and ASAP 2.0 train (ELL bands)", "tier": "n/a", "rows": [{"corpus": "ELLIPSE", "measure": "CV (shipped)", "high": 0.4555, "low": 0.5309, "cohen_d_low_minus_high": 0.467}, {"corpus": "ELLIPSE", "measure": "raw SD", "high": 11.1692, "low": 19.0752, "cohen_d_low_minus_high": 0.568}, {"corpus": "ELLIPSE", "measure": "median abs dev", "high": 6.5034, "low": 11.2131, "cohen_d_low_minus_high": 0.486}, {"corpus": "ELLIPSE", "measure": "normalised entropy", "high": 0.9632, "low": 0.9303, "cohen_d_low_minus_high": -0.647}, {"corpus": "ELLIPSE", "measure": "range/mean", "high": 1.7795, "low": 1.8188, "cohen_d_low_minus_high": 0.059}, {"corpus": "ASAP", "measure": "CV (shipped)", "nonELL": 0.4458, "ELL": 0.4606, "cohen_d_ELL_minus_nonELL": 0.119}, {"corpus": "ASAP", "measure": "raw SD", "nonELL": 9.404, "ELL": 11.6074, "cohen_d_ELL_minus_nonELL": 0.306}, {"corpus": "ASAP", "measure": "median abs dev", "nonELL": 5.7192, "ELL": 7.1405, "cohen_d_ELL_minus_nonELL": 0.247}, {"corpus": "ASAP", "measure": "normalised entropy", "nonELL": 0.9625, "ELL": 0.9538, "cohen_d_ELL_minus_nonELL": -0.292}, {"corpus": "ASAP", "measure": "range/mean", "nonELL": 1.685, "ELL": 1.6269, "cohen_d_ELL_minus_nonELL": -0.108}], "note": "FOUR OF FIVE dispersion measures agree, on BOTH corpora and in the same direction: the more fluent group (higher proficiency in ELLIPSE, non-ELL in ASAP) writes sentences of LESS variable length. CV, raw standard deviation and median absolute deviation all say so directly; normalised entropy says it with the opposite sign because entropy over length proportions is MAXIMISED by uniformity. Only range/mean fails (d +0.059 and -0.108), and it is a max-minus-min statistic dominated by a single outlier sentence. So the disparity is a property of the WRITING, not of untell's CV formula, and any burstiness signal built on a standard dispersion measure inherits it. Effect sizes are smaller on ASAP (0.12-0.31) than ELLIPSE (0.47-0.65), matching the smaller measured disparity there (1.21x vs 1.33x). METHOD NOTE: this run EXCLUDES essays with fewer than 2 sentences, where dispersion is undefined; the shipped detector returns 0.0 for them. That is why ELLIPSE CV reads 0.5309 here against 0.5214 in ellipse-raw-signal-effect-sizes, which included the degenerate cases."}
{"recipe": "true-ngram-perplexity-contrast", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "45a3df8", "kind": "a THIRD signal family: real n-gram perplexity, not the stoplist proxy", "model": "interpolated bigram/unigram LM (lambda=0.7, add-1 unigram backoff) trained on NLTK Brown + Reuters via raw.githubusercontent: 2,001,501 tokens, 56,424 types, 675,817 bigrams. Independent of both essay corpora. Pure stdlib, no HuggingFace.", "rows": [{"corpus": "ELLIPSE", "groups": "low-prof vs high-prof", "mean_logppl_low": 6.5816, "mean_logppl_high": 6.4406, "cohen_d_high_minus_low": -0.32, "lower_perplexity_group": "high-prof"}, {"corpus": "ASAP", "groups": "ELL vs non-ELL", "mean_logppl_ELL": 7.0837, "mean_logppl_nonELL": 6.8778, "cohen_d_nonELL_minus_ELL": -0.491, "lower_perplexity_group": "nonELL"}], "note": "THE RESULT THAT REVISES THE EARLIER STORY. Real perplexity points the SAME way as burstiness and the OPPOSITE way to untell's common-word-ratio proxy. More fluent writers (high proficiency, non-ELL) produce more PREDICTABLE English, so a genuine LM assigns them LOWER perplexity, which is the 'more machine-like' end. So across three signal families: common-word-ratio penalises LOW proficiency (the Liang-shaped direction), while burstiness AND true perplexity both penalise the MORE FLUENT writer. Two of three point at the more fluent writer. CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS REPO: untell's lite 'perplexity' channel is a 120-word stoplist ratio, and on this population it is ANTI-CORRELATED with actual perplexity. It is not a valid stand-in for the thing it is named after, and the earlier decomposition note that read it as 'the perplexity account behaving as predicted' was reading the proxy, not perplexity. LIMITATION, and it is a real one: the LM is 1961 Brown American English plus Reuters newswire, a domain mismatch with school essays. Non-native writing being less predictable to THAT model is partly expected. A modern in-domain LM could differ, and testing one needs weights this environment's egress policy blocks."}
{"recipe": "gpt2-transformer-perplexity-contrast", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "8fa7279", "kind": "THE OPEN QUESTION, ANSWERED: does a real transformer show the same correlation", "model": "GPT-2 124M, gpt2-lm-head-10.onnx (664,871,060 bytes) from the onnx/models LFS store via media.githubusercontent.com; BPE from OpenAI's published encoder.json + vocab.bpe on GitHub; onnxruntime 1.29.0 from pypi. NO HuggingFace anywhere in the path.", "rows": [{"corpus": "ASAP", "groups": "ELL vs non-ELL", "n_per_group": 250, "mean_nll_ELL": 3.8748, "mean_nll_nonELL": 3.5533, "cohen_d_nonELL_minus_ELL": -0.671, "lower_perplexity": "nonELL"}, {"corpus": "ELLIPSE", "groups": "low vs high proficiency", "n_per_group": 250, "mean_nll_low": 3.7733, "mean_nll_high": 3.1725, "cohen_d_high_minus_low": -1.32, "lower_perplexity": "high-prof"}], "note": "ANSWERS the question left open all session. A real 124M-parameter transformer shows the SAME correlation as the bigram LM, and shows it MORE STRONGLY: d -0.671 vs -0.491 on ASAP, and -1.320 vs -0.320 on ELLIPSE, the last of which is a very large effect. Native and more proficient writers produce text a real language model finds MORE predictable, i.e. lower perplexity, i.e. the machine-like end. So the direction is not an artifact of a weak n-gram model over 1961 newswire -- scaling the language model AMPLIFIES it. Any perplexity-based detector, which is most neural detectors, should be expected to flag the more fluent writer more on this population until measured otherwise. HOW IT WAS OBTAINED, because I twice reported it as blocked: huggingface.co and four mirrors are egress-blocked, but the ONNX model zoo keeps GPT-2 in GitHub LFS, and LFS objects for public repos are served by media.githubusercontent.com, which is reachable. The earlier 403 was a github.com URL hitting this session's repo scoping, not an egress denial -- I read one error as the other and stopped. LIMITS: n=250 per group (sampled, seed 0), 384-token cap per essay, GPT-2 small rather than a detector. This is a perplexity SIGNAL, not a detector verdict."}
{"recipe": "pelic-l1-and-level", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "fa50650", "kind": "THIRD corpus, new axis (first language), and a DIRECTION REVERSAL", "corpus": "PELIC (Pittsburgh English Language Institute Corpus), 17,144 texts >=60 words of 46,204 total. ADULT university ESL, 20+ first languages, classroom tasks. Fetched from GitHub LFS. Different age band, different task and different L1 mix from ELLIPSE and ASAP.", "tier": "lite", "threshold": 0.5, "overall_fpr": 0.391, "rows": [{"axis": "L1", "note": "extremes are small-n", "worst": "German", "worst_fpr": 0.596, "n_worst": 52, "best": "Hebrew", "best_fpr": 0.262, "n_best": 61, "ratio": 2.27, "separated": true}, {"axis": "L1", "note": "ROBUST large-n pair", "worst": "Arabic", "worst_fpr": 0.409, "n_worst": 5818, "best": "Turkish", "best_fpr": 0.297, "n_best": 536, "ratio": 1.38, "separated": true}, {"axis": "level_id", "levels": {"2": 0.529, "3": 0.409, "4": 0.399, "5": 0.365}, "ns": {"2": 102, "3": 3571, "4": 8217, "5": 5254}, "ratio": 1.45, "separated": true, "direction": "monotonically DECREASING with proficiency level"}, {"axis": "gender", "male": 0.397, "female": 0.384, "ratio": 1.03, "separated": false}], "note": "NEW AXIS: first language changes the false-positive rate. Arabic 40.9% (n=5818) vs Turkish 29.7% (n=536), 1.38x, separated -- both well sampled. The 2.27x German-vs-Hebrew extreme rests on n=52 and n=61 and should not be the quoted figure. DIRECTION REVERSAL, and it must not be smoothed over: PELIC's proficiency effect runs the OPPOSITE way to ELLIPSE's. Here FPR falls monotonically as level rises (52.9% at level 2 to 36.5% at level 5, 1.45x, separated); in ELLIPSE it ROSE with proficiency (33.2% -> 44.2%). So the direction of the proficiency disparity is NOT a property of the detector alone -- it depends on the population and the task. ELLIPSE is US school students writing full independent essays; PELIC is adult university ESL classroom writing. This strengthens rather than weakens the case for the instrument: you cannot predict who a detector fails from a published result, even a result about the same detector and the same kind of subgroup. It has to be measured on the population that will actually be judged. Gender does not separate here either, consistent with ELLIPSE and ASAP. CAVEAT: PELIC's median text is 23 words, so the >=60-word filter keeps 37% and may select for longer, more elaborated answers."}
{"recipe": "published-vs-student-fpr", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "d78b9ba", "kind": "THE LARGEST DISPARITY FOUND, and it is not demographic", "tier": "lite", "threshold": 0.5, "rows": [{"population": "Gutenberg literary classics", "n": 144, "fpr": 0.014, "ci": [0.004, 0.049]}, {"population": "Brown 1961 published American prose", "n": 280, "fpr": 0.032, "ci": [0.017, 0.06]}, {"population": "Reuters newswire", "n": 26, "fpr": 0.038, "ci": [0.007, 0.189]}, {"population": "ASAP US school students", "n": 17307, "fpr": 0.316}, {"population": "ELLIPSE US school ESL", "n": 3904, "fpr": 0.387}, {"population": "PELIC adult university ESL", "n": 17144, "fpr": 0.391}], "note": "At one threshold, published professional prose scores 1.4-3.8% while student writing scores 31.6-39.1% -- roughly a TENFOLD gap, an order of magnitude larger than any subgroup disparity measured in this whole body of work (which topped out at 2.27x, and 1.55x among well-sampled groups). The practical reading: a detector validated on 'human writing' -- which in practice usually means published, edited, professional text -- will look excellent and then fail catastrophically on students, who are the population it is actually deployed against. This is a plausible mechanism for vendors reporting sub-1% false-positive rates that nobody can reproduce in a classroom. CONFOUNDS, and they are substantial: Brown/Gutenberg/Reuters are professionally edited and copyedited, drawn from different genres and registers, and chunked into 350-word slices of longer works, whereas the student corpora are first-draft responses to assigned prompts. Genre, editing and task all differ alongside 'professional vs student', so this is not a clean single-variable contrast. Reuters n=26 has a very wide interval and should not be quoted alone. What the comparison does establish is that the population a detector is validated on can differ from its deployment population by an order of magnitude in false-positive rate."}
{"recipe": "published-vs-student-threshold-sweep", "date": "2026-09-01", "head": "ff4fd20", "kind": "does the professional-vs-student gap hold at the SHIPPED threshold", "tier": "lite", "rows": [{"threshold": 0.3, "brown": 0.25, "gutenberg": 0.472, "ellipse_students": 0.974}, {"threshold": 0.5, "brown": 0.032, "gutenberg": 0.014, "ellipse_students": 0.387}, {"threshold": 0.7, "brown": 0.0, "gutenberg": 0.0, "ellipse_students": 0.032}], "n": {"brown": 280, "gutenberg": 144}, "note": "Yes, and it is starker at the shipped 0.30 threshold than at 0.50. Published prose 25.0% (Brown) and 47.2% (Gutenberg) against 97.4% for student ESL essays. At 0.70 professional prose is at 0.0% while students are still at 3.2%. The gap is present at every operating point tested, so it is not an artifact of threshold choice. Gutenberg scoring worse than Brown at 0.30 is worth noting rather than explaining away: 19th-century literary prose is a genre of its own and the ordering among professional corpora is not stable, only their distance from student writing is."}
